LO, SF, SC, CV = "Lodi", "Sierra Foothills", "Southern California", "Central Valley"
regions = [
 region("us", "California", 36.8, -120.5, 5.6,
 "Four of every five bottles of American wine, from Mission-grape beginnings to the most expensive Cabernet on earth.",
 "California makes roughly 80 percent of all US wine and would rank among the world's largest producers if it were a country. It has 154 of the 280 American AVAs. Its wine geography is a contest between the cold Pacific and the hot interior. Along the coast, the cold California Current and the Coast Ranges create fog belts and wind gaps; where valleys open to the sea (Carneros, the Petaluma Gap, the Salinas Valley, the Santa Ynez Valley) Pinot Noir and Chardonnay thrive. Behind the coastal mountains, Napa, Sonoma's inland valleys and Paso Robles give Cabernet, Zinfandel and Rhône varieties. The vast Central Valley, irrigated and hot, grows most of the state's tonnage for everyday wine, while Lodi and the Sierra Foothills keep some of the world's oldest Zinfandel vines.\n\nThe industry is in a correction. After years of falling US consumption and a glut of grapes, growers pulled tens of thousands of acres from 2023 onward. The preliminary 2025 crush was 2.76 million tons, down 6.2 percent from 2024 and the smallest in about two decades, with an average price of 979 dollars a ton. OIV puts US vineyard area at 415,000 ha in 2025, the seventh straight year of decline, mainly because of uprooting in California.",
 "Franciscan friars brought the Mission grape (Listán Prieto) north from Mexico; Junípero Serra is traditionally credited with the first planting at Mission San Diego de Alcalá in 1769, and Mission San Gabriel became the mother vineyard of the south. Jean-Louis Vignes, a Bordelais, imported French vines to Los Angeles in the 1830s. The Gold Rush (1848) created a market, Agoston Haraszthy's Buena Vista (1857) and 1861 vine-collecting trip widened the range of varieties, and by the 1880s California wines were winning medals in Paris. Phylloxera, then Prohibition (1920 to 1933), wrecked the industry; growers survived by shipping grapes east for legal home winemaking, which favoured thick-skinned Alicante Bouschet over fine varieties.\n\nRecovery was slow until the 1960s. UC Davis (Maynard Amerine and Albert Winkler's heat-summation regions, 1944) supplied science; Robert Mondavi (1966) supplied ambition; the 1976 Judgment of Paris supplied fame. The 1980s and 1990s brought the AXR1 phylloxera disaster and replanting, the rise of cult Cabernets and a Rhône movement. Since 2017 wildfire and smoke, drought and heat have become the defining risks.",
 "Mediterranean climate: dry summers, wet winters. Heat summation runs from Region I (cool coast) to Region V (Central Valley). Soils range from volcanic to marine sedimentary, granitic and alluvial. Most quality vineyards are drip-irrigated.",
 "Cabernet Sauvignon and Chardonnay are the most planted wine grapes, followed by Pinot Noir, Zinfandel, Merlot, French Colombard, Rubired, Sauvignon Blanc, Syrah and Pinot Gris.",
 "Everything from bulk wine and fortified wine to world-famous Cabernet, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and sparkling wine.",
 "AVAs under federal law (85 percent of grapes from the AVA); a wine labeled 'California' must be 100 percent California grapes. County names are also legal appellations (75 percent). State law requires conjunctive labeling for Napa, Sonoma, Paso Robles and Lodi sub-AVAs.",
 ["North Coast", "Napa Valley", "Sonoma County", "Mendocino County", "Lake County", "Central Coast", "Santa Barbara County", "Paso Robles", "Lodi", "Sierra Foothills", "Southern California", "Central Valley"], SRC, parent=None),
 region("us", LO, 38.17, -121.30, 9.4,
 "Old, own-rooted Zinfandel on sandy river soils: the grower capital of California.",
 "Lodi, in the northern San Joaquin Valley between Sacramento and Stockton, grows a large share of California's premium wine grapes, much of it sold to wineries elsewhere. Afternoon breezes from San Francisco Bay through the Sacramento-San Joaquin Delta cool it more than the Central Valley to the south. Its glory is old-vine Zinfandel, head-trained and often own-rooted in deep, sandy soils where phylloxera struggles to survive. Lodi also grows Cinsault, Carignan and a growing list of Iberian and German varieties.",
 "German farmers who moved from the Dakotas settled Lodi in the late 19th century and planted Zinfandel and Flame Tokay table grapes. Many families still farm the same land. The Bechthold Vineyard's Cinsault was planted in 1886. The Lodi AVA was approved in 1986 and expanded in 2002, and in 2006 TTB created seven sub-AVAs. Lodi Rules, launched in 2005, was California's first third-party certified sustainable winegrowing programme.",
 "Deep sandy loam on the Mokelumne River fan in the west; clay and cobbly soils in the eastern foothills (Clements Hills, Borden Ranch). Delta breezes.",
 "Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Merlot, Cinsault, Carignan, Petite Sirah, Albariño, Tempranillo.",
 "Ripe, spicy Zinfandel; light, perfumed old-vine Cinsault; everyday varietals.",
 "Lodi AVA with seven sub-AVAs (2006); sub-AVA labels must also carry 'Lodi'.",
 ["Lodi", "Alta Mesa", "Borden Ranch", "Clements Hills", "Cosumnes River", "Jahant", "Mokelumne River", "Sloughhouse"], SRC, parent="California"),
 region("us", SF, 38.5, -120.8, 8.0,
 "Gold Rush country, where miners planted Zinfandel and some of the vines are still alive.",
 "The Sierra Foothills AVA follows the western slopes of the Sierra Nevada through eight counties from Yuba south to Mariposa, mostly between 300 and 900 m. Summers are hot and dry, nights cool, and soils are decomposed granite and volcanic loam. Amador County's Shenandoah Valley is the heart of old-vine Zinfandel; El Dorado and Fair Play climb higher for Rhône, Barbera and cooler-climate varieties.",
 "Miners and the merchants who fed them planted vines during the Gold Rush of the 1850s; by the 1890s there were more than a hundred wineries. Prohibition and the end of mining emptied the hills, but some old Zinfandel survived, including the Original Grandpère vineyard in Amador, claimed to have been planted before 1869. Sutter Home's Amador Zinfandel from Deaver Vineyard (1968) brought the region back to attention, and a stuck fermentation of Zinfandel at Sutter Home in 1975 gave the world White Zinfandel.",
 "Decomposed granite and volcanic soils, elevations of 300 to 1,000 m, hot days and cool nights.",
 "Zinfandel, Barbera, Syrah, Petite Sirah, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Sangiovese, Cabernet Sauvignon.",
 "Full, spicy Zinfandel and juicy Barbera; Rhône blends from higher elevations.",
 "Sierra Foothills umbrella AVA with five sub-AVAs.",
 ["Sierra Foothills", "El Dorado", "Fair Play", "California Shenandoah Valley", "Fiddletown", "North Yuba"], SRC, parent="California"),
 region("us", SC, 33.9, -117.6, 7.6,
 "Where California wine began, then vanished under freeways: missions, Cucamonga's old vines, Temecula's wineries and Malibu's hillsides.",
 "Southern California was the first wine region of the state. Mission San Gabriel (founded 1771) became the mother vineyard; Los Angeles was the wine capital of California in the 1840s and 1850s; and the Cucamonga Valley east of LA had more than 20,000 acres of vines in the early 20th century. Urban growth and Pierce's disease erased most of it.\n\nToday the main centre is Temecula Valley, with dozens of tourist-oriented wineries, plus small AVAs from the Malibu hills to the high desert and San Diego County, where two new AVAs were approved in 2024 and two more (Rancho Guejito, Rancho Santa Fe) were proposed in 2024 and 2026.",
 "Jean-Louis Vignes planted French vines in Los Angeles in the 1830s. Anaheim was founded in 1857 by German winegrowers; its vineyards were wiped out in the 1880s by a mysterious vine disease, later identified as Pierce's disease. Secondo Guasti's Italian Vineyard Company at Cucamonga (from 1900) claimed to be the largest vineyard in the world. In 1999 to 2000 the glassy-winged sharpshooter spread Pierce's disease through Temecula.",
 "Warm Mediterranean to semi-arid climate, tempered by marine air through coastal gaps; sandy and decomposed granite soils.",
 "Zinfandel, Mission, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo, Grenache, Viognier, Sangiovese.",
 "Rhône and Mediterranean reds; old-vine Zinfandel; rosé.",
 "South Coast umbrella AVA plus independent AVAs in Los Angeles, San Bernardino and Kern counties.",
 ["South Coast", "Temecula Valley", "Cucamonga Valley", "Malibu Coast", "Saddle Rock-Malibu", "Malibu-Newton Canyon", "Palos Verdes Peninsula", "Ramona Valley", "San Pasqual Valley", "San Luis Rey", "Yucaipa Valley", "Leona Valley", "Sierra Pelona Valley", "Antelope Valley of the California High Desert", "Tehachapi Mountains"], SRC, parent="California"),
 region("us", CV, 37.0, -120.3, 6.8,
 "The irrigated heartland where most California wine by volume is grown.",
 "The Central Valley (the Sacramento and San Joaquin valleys) is one of the world's most productive farm regions, and its hot, irrigated vineyards supply most of California's wine by volume, along with table grapes and raisins. Big producers such as E. & J. Gallo (Modesto) are based here. A handful of AVAs cover the cooler foothill margins and the Delta.",
 "Large-scale planting followed irrigation projects in the late 19th and 20th centuries. After Prohibition the valley made fortified 'port' and 'sherry' and later jug wine. Madera became known for fortified Muscat and port-style wines (Ficklin, Quady).",
 "Deep alluvial soils, very hot summers, irrigation from Sierra snowmelt.",
 "French Colombard, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Rubired, Muscat, Merlot.",
 "Everyday wine, fortified and sweet Muscats.",
 "Few AVAs; most wine is labeled California.",
 ["Madera", "Diablo Grande", "Salado Creek", "Tracy Hills", "Paulsell Valley", "River Junction", "Squaw Valley-Miramonte"], SRC, parent="California")]

A = []
A.append(ava("Lodi", LO, "Lodi (1986, expanded 2002) is a grower's appellation of about 540,000 acres and roughly 100,000 acres of vines in San Joaquin and Sacramento counties. It is California's Zinfandel capital, with hundreds of acres of vines more than 50 and some more than 100 years old, often own-rooted in sand. Cooling Delta breezes keep it fresher than the rest of the Central Valley.\n\nThe 'Lodi Native' project has bottled single-vineyard old-vine Zinfandels with minimal intervention to show site differences, and producers from Turley to Bedrock and Michael David buy Lodi old vines.", sub="Central Valley", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon, Cinsault, Carignan, Petite Sirah, Chardonnay", producers=["Michael David", "Bokisch", "Klinker Brick", "m2", "St. Amant", "Turley (sourcing)", "Bedrock (sourcing)"], contains=["Alta Mesa", "Borden Ranch", "Clements Hills", "Cosumnes River", "Jahant", "Mokelumne River", "Sloughhouse"]))
A.append(ava("Mokelumne River", LO, "The largest and most important Lodi sub-AVA (2006), on the deep, sandy alluvial fan of the Mokelumne River west of the foothills. It holds most of Lodi's oldest Zinfandel and Cinsault, own-rooted because phylloxera cannot thrive in such sand. Bechthold, Soucie and Royal Tee are famous old-vine sites.", sub="Lodi", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Cinsault, Carignan, Cabernet Sauvignon", producers=["Turley (Kirschenmann)", "Bedrock", "St. Amant", "Michael David"]))
A.append(ava("Clements Hills", LO, "Rolling hills (2006) in the eastern part of Lodi, with red, cobbly clay-loam soils on old river terraces; slightly warmer than the west. Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel and Iberian varieties.", sub="Lodi", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo, Albariño", producers=["Bokisch", "Klinker Brick"]))
A.append(ava("Borden Ranch", LO, "Hilly eastern Lodi AVA (2006) of cobbled, gravelly old river terraces at the foot of the Sierra, on the old Borden ranch. Bordeaux varieties and Zinfandel.", sub="Lodi", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Petite Sirah"))
A.append(ava("Alta Mesa", LO, "A high, flat terrace (2006) in the northeastern part of Lodi, in Sacramento County, with shallow sandy loam over hardpan that limits vine vigour. It is warmer than western Lodi because it lies farther from the Delta breezes, and it grows Zinfandel and Rhône varieties.", sub="Lodi", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon"))
A.append(ava("Cosumnes River", LO, "The northwestern Lodi sub-AVA (2006) on the Cosumnes River, close to the Delta and among the coolest, with heavy clay and alluvial soils; Pinot Grigio, Chardonnay and Zinfandel.", sub="Lodi", styles=["white", "red"], dom="red", grapes="Chardonnay, Pinot Grigio, Zinfandel"))
A.append(ava("Jahant", LO, "A small Lodi sub-AVA (2006) between the Mokelumne and Cosumnes rivers, close to the Delta, with clay-loam soils and cool afternoon winds. Zinfandel, Cabernet and Petite Sirah.", sub="Lodi", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah"))
A.append(ava("Sloughhouse", LO, "The northeastern and warmest Lodi sub-AVA (2006), in Sacramento County in the Sierra foothills along the Cosumnes River, with red, cobbly soils. Zinfandel and Rhône varieties.", sub="Lodi", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Syrah, Petite Sirah"))
A.append(ava("Sierra Foothills", SF, "A long umbrella AVA (1987) of about 2.6 million acres following the Sierra Nevada foothills through eight counties. Gold Rush vineyards of Zinfandel survive here, and the higher reaches grow Rhône varieties and Barbera.", sub="California", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Barbera, Syrah, Petite Sirah, Sangiovese", contains=["El Dorado", "Fair Play", "California Shenandoah Valley", "Fiddletown", "North Yuba"]))
A.append(ava("California Shenandoah Valley", SF, "The core of Amador County wine (1983), a valley of rolling hills around Plymouth at 300 to 600 m, with decomposed granite and volcanic soils. It holds some of the oldest Zinfandel vines in California, including the Original Grandpère vineyard claimed to date from before 1869, and Deaver Vineyard, whose fruit made Sutter Home's 1968 Amador Zinfandel. Barbera and Rhône varieties also do well.\n\nThe name includes 'California' to separate it from the older Shenandoah Valley AVA of Virginia and West Virginia.", sub="Sierra Foothills", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Barbera, Syrah, Primitivo, Sangiovese", producers=["Sobon Estate", "Terre Rouge", "Turley (Amador)", "Andis", "Vino Noceto", "Easton"], match=["Shenandoah Valley (California)"]))
A.append(ava("Fiddletown", SF, "A small AVA (1983) in Amador County above the Shenandoah Valley, at about 450 to 760 m, with old Zinfandel vineyards such as Eschen, planted around 1910.", sub="Sierra Foothills", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel", producers=["Easton (Eschen Vineyard)", "Ridge (historic Fiddletown Zinfandel)"]))
A.append(ava("El Dorado", SF, "A high AVA (1983) in El Dorado County, near where gold was found at Coloma in 1848, with vineyards from about 365 to 1,070 m, among the highest in California. Volcanic and granitic soils and cool nights suit Rhône varieties, Barbera, Zinfandel and even Riesling.", sub="Sierra Foothills", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Syrah, Barbera, Grenache, Cabernet Franc", producers=["Lava Cap", "Boeger", "Madroña", "Holly's Hill"], contains=["Fair Play"]))
A.append(ava("Fair Play", SF, "A high sub-AVA (2001, expanded 2015) around the hamlet of Fair Play in southern El Dorado County, at about 600 to 900 m on decomposed granite. Small family wineries make Rhône and Spanish varieties and Zinfandel.", sub="El Dorado", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Grenache, Tempranillo, Zinfandel, Barbera", producers=["Skinner", "Cedarville", "Miraflores", "Fitzpatrick"]))
A.append(ava("North Yuba", SF, "A small, high foothill AVA (1985) in Yuba County, created around Renaissance Vineyard, a winery founded by a spiritual community in the 1970s and known for long-aged Cabernet and late-harvest whites.", sub="Sierra Foothills", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc", producers=["Renaissance Vineyard"]))
A.append(ava("South Coast", SC, "Umbrella AVA (1985) of about 2.1 million acres covering the coastal portions of Orange, Riverside and San Diego counties, including Temecula Valley, Ramona Valley, San Pasqual Valley and San Luis Rey. Two further AVAs inside it were proposed by TTB: Rancho Guejito (2024) and Rancho Santa Fe (August 2026).", sub="California", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo, Viognier, Zinfandel", contains=["Temecula Valley", "Ramona Valley", "San Pasqual Valley", "San Luis Rey"]))
A.append(ava("Temecula Valley", SC, "Southern California's main wine district (1984), in Riverside County about 35 km from the Pacific. Cool air comes through the Rainbow Gap and Santa Margarita River gap each afternoon, and elevations of 300 to 450 m moderate the heat. Ely Callaway planted here in 1969; today there are dozens of wineries, mostly tourist-oriented, growing Rhône, Italian and Spanish varieties. Pierce's disease, spread by the glassy-winged sharpshooter, devastated vineyards around 1999 to 2000.", sub="South Coast", styles=["red", "white", "sparkling"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Tempranillo, Sangiovese, Viognier, Cabernet Sauvignon", producers=["Callaway", "South Coast Winery", "Wilson Creek", "Hart"]))
A.append(ava("Ramona Valley", SC, "An upland AVA (2006) of about 89,000 acres in San Diego County, at 300 to 900 m around the town of Ramona. At approval it had 17 vineyards on about 45 acres; many small wineries now grow Rhône and Bordeaux varieties.", sub="South Coast", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo"))
A.append(ava("San Pasqual Valley", SC, "One of the earliest AVAs (1981), a small valley near Escondido in San Diego County. Its vineyards have mostly disappeared, but it remains on the TTB list. The proposed Rancho Guejito AVA would partly overlap it.", sub="South Coast", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc"))
A.append(ava("San Luis Rey", SC, "A new AVA (2024) of about 97,700 acres in northern San Diego County around Fallbrook, Bonsall, Pala and Pauma Valley, named for the San Luis Rey River and Mission San Luis Rey de Francia (1798). At approval it had 44 vineyards on about 256 acres and 23 wineries. Marine air reaches the valleys through the river corridor.", sub="South Coast", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Grenache, Sangiovese"))
A.append(ava("Cucamonga Valley", SC, "A historic AVA (1995) in San Bernardino and Riverside counties, east of Los Angeles. In 1900 Secondo Guasti founded the Italian Vineyard Company here and planted about 5,000 acres, then billed as the largest vineyard in the world; by the 1920s the valley had more than 20,000 acres of vines. Warehouses, freeways and Ontario airport have replaced most of them, but scattered old, dry-farmed Zinfandel and Mission vines survive, some more than a century old, and Galleano Winery (1927) still makes wine from them.", sub="Southern California", styles=["red", "fortified"], dom="red", grapes="Zinfandel, Mission, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Palomino", producers=["Galleano", "Joseph Filippi", "Bedrock (Cucamonga Zinfandel)"]))
A.append(ava("Malibu Coast", SC, "An AVA (2014) of about 44,590 acres in the Santa Monica Mountains of Los Angeles and Ventura counties, with about 52 small vineyards on 198 acres at approval. Ocean views and marine air, but also the constant threat of wildfire: the 2018 Woolsey Fire burned through many vineyards. Los Angeles County has since restricted new vineyard planting in the Santa Monica Mountains.", sub="Southern California", styles=["red", "white", "rose"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir", contains=["Saddle Rock-Malibu", "Malibu-Newton Canyon"]))
A.append(ava("Saddle Rock-Malibu", SC, "A tiny AVA (2006) of about 2,090 acres in the Santa Monica Mountains above Malibu, at 520 to 680 m, essentially the Semler family's Malibu Family Wines vineyard (planted 1997). Its hilltop vines produce Bordeaux and Rhône reds.", sub="Malibu Coast", styles=["red", "rose"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot", producers=["Malibu Family Wines (Saddlerock)"]))
A.append(ava("Malibu-Newton Canyon", SC, "A bowl-shaped AVA (1996) of about 850 acres high in the Santa Monica Mountains, created around Rosenthal's vineyard, which was planted in 1987. Above the marine layer, it grows Cabernet Sauvignon and Chardonnay.", sub="Malibu Coast", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay", producers=["Rosenthal"]))
A.append(ava("Palos Verdes Peninsula", SC, "A small coastal AVA (2021) of about 15,900 acres on the hilly peninsula south of Los Angeles, with only about 7 acres of vines at approval. Uplifted marine terraces and constant ocean breezes.", sub="Southern California", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay"))
A.append(ava("Yucaipa Valley", SC, "A new AVA (2024) of 36,467 acres in San Bernardino County, in the foothills of the San Bernardino Mountains, with about 23 vineyards and two wineries at approval. Elevations of 600 to 1,500 m and cool canyon winds set it apart from the hot inland valleys.", sub="Southern California", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Zinfandel"))
A.append(ava("Leona Valley", SC, "A narrow valley (2008) in northern Los Angeles County that follows the San Andreas fault, at about 900 m, with cold winters and hot summers. About 20 acres of vines at approval.", sub="Southern California", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot"))
A.append(ava("Sierra Pelona Valley", SC, "A small valley (2010) in northern Los Angeles County near Agua Dulce, at about 760 to 1,000 m, with about 96 acres of vines at approval. A high-desert-edge climate with big temperature swings.", sub="Southern California", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Zinfandel"))
A.append(ava("Antelope Valley of the California High Desert", SC, "A large AVA (2011) in the western Mojave Desert in Los Angeles and Kern counties, at around 700 to 900 m. Hot days, cold nights, strong winds and very low rainfall; vineyards are small and irrigated.", sub="Southern California", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Syrah"))
A.append(ava("Tehachapi Mountains", SC, "A high mountain AVA (2020) of about 58,000 acres in Kern County, at roughly 1,000 to 1,500 m, between the San Joaquin Valley and the Mojave. Cool nights and big temperature swings; six small vineyards and one winery at approval.", sub="Southern California", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Pinot Noir"))
A.append(ava("Madera", CV, "A warm Central Valley AVA (1985) in Madera and Fresno counties, known for fortified and sweet wines: Ficklin pioneered port-style wines from Portuguese varieties in 1946, and Andrew Quady's Essensia (orange Muscat) and Elysium (black Muscat) are dessert-wine classics.", sub="Central Valley", styles=["fortified", "sweet", "red", "white"], dom="fortified", grapes="Touriga Nacional, Tinta Cão, Orange Muscat, Black Muscat, French Colombard", producers=["Quady", "Ficklin"]))
A.append(ava("Diablo Grande", CV, "A single-development AVA (1998) in the Diablo Range foothills west of Patterson, Stanislaus County, created alongside a planned resort and golf community. It is cooler than the San Joaquin Valley floor below, but its vineyards are small and few wines use the name.", sub="Central Valley", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah"))
A.append(ava("Salado Creek", CV, "A small AVA (2004) of about 2,940 acres at the foot of the Diablo Range in western Stanislaus County, with 44 acres of vines at approval. Spanish explorer Gabriel Moraga named Salado ('salty') Creek.", sub="Central Valley", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot"))
A.append(ava("Tracy Hills", CV, "An AVA (2006) of about 39,200 acres in the foothills southwest of Tracy, in San Joaquin and Stanislaus counties, with about 1,005 acres of vines at approval; afternoon winds from the Altamont Pass cool the area.", sub="Central Valley", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay"))
A.append(ava("Paulsell Valley", CV, "A foothill AVA (2022) of about 34,155 acres in eastern Stanislaus County, in rolling grazing land at the edge of the Sierra foothills east of Modesto. The petition stressed its rolling topography and soils, distinct from the flat, irrigated valley floor to the west.", sub="Central Valley", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel"))
A.append(ava("River Junction", CV, "A small AVA (2001) at the confluence of the Stanislaus and San Joaquin rivers in San Joaquin County, defined by river-borne alluvial soils and Delta breezes. It was created around a single large vineyard, and the name is rarely seen on labels.", sub="Central Valley", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white", grapes="Chardonnay, Merlot"))
A.append(ava("Squaw Valley-Miramonte", CV, "A foothill AVA (2015) of about 44,690 acres in eastern Fresno County, at 450 to 1,000 m on the western slope of the Sierra, much cooler than the San Joaquin Valley floor. Three wineries and a few vineyards at approval.", sub="Central Valley", styles=["red"], dom="red", grapes="Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel"))

S = []
S.append(site("mission-san-gabriel", "us", "Mission San Gabriel Arcángel (Trinity Vine)", "historic", "California (no AVA)", 34.0967, -118.1069,
 "Founded in 1771, San Gabriel became the 'mother vineyard' of southern California, supplying Mission grape cuttings and wine. A vine in the mission garden, known as the Trinity vine, is traditionally said to be descended from the original planting and is often called the oldest living Mission vine in California.", cls="Founded 1771"))
S.append(site("mission-san-diego", "us", "Mission San Diego de Alcalá", "historic", "South Coast", 32.7855, -117.1065,
 "Founded by Junípero Serra in July 1769, the first of California's 21 missions. Tradition credits it with the first planting of the Mission grape in what is now California; some historians favour San Juan Capistrano or San Gabriel for the first vintage.", cls="Founded 1769"))
S.append(site("guasti", "us", "Guasti (Italian Vineyard Company)", "historic", "Cucamonga Valley", 34.0650, -117.5864,
 "Secondo Guasti founded the Italian Vineyard Company here in 1900 and built a company town with a church and school. At its peak the vineyard covered about 5,000 acres and was promoted as the largest in the world. The old winery buildings survive next to Ontario airport.", cls="Founded 1900"))
S.append(site("galleano", "us", "Galleano Winery", "estate", "Cucamonga Valley", 34.0097, -117.5456,
 "Founded in 1927 by Domenico Galleano, a family winery that still farms and buys old, dry-farmed Zinfandel and Mission vines in the Cucamonga Valley, a living remnant of what was once the largest wine district in California.", producers=["Galleano"]))
S.append(site("bechthold", "us", "Bechthold Vineyard", "vineyard", "Mokelumne River", 38.1430, -121.3200,
 "Joseph Spenker planted this Cinsault in 1886 on Lodi's sandy soils, and the own-rooted, head-trained vines are among the oldest Cinsault in the world. His descendants, the Bechthold family, still own it; Michael David farms it and it supplies producers from Turley to Bedrock.", q="approx", producers=["Michael David", "Turley", "Bedrock"]))
S.append(site("sobon-estate", "us", "Sobon Estate (D'Agostini winery, 1856)", "historic", "California Shenandoah Valley", 38.5324, -120.7555,
 "Adam Uhlinger founded a winery here in 1856, later the D'Agostini Winery and one of the oldest in California. Sobon Estate now runs it, with a museum of Gold Rush-era winemaking in the old stone cellar.", cls="Founded 1856", producers=["Sobon Estate"]))
save_part("p05_ca_rest", regions=regions, appellations=A, sites=S)
